<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lombard League</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> League was an alliance of cities in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> formed in 1167 to resist the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. Its forces defeated Barbarossa at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnano">Battle of Legnano</a> in 1176. The league secured substantial self-government for the Italian cities, limiting imperial power in Italy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Frederick Barbarossa's Italian campaigns of the twelfth century showed both what this arrangement could do and where it failed: he could assemble strong armies but could not sustain them against the organised resistance of the <b>Lombard League</b>, which defeated him at Legnano in 1176.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Lombard League</b> defeated Frederick Barbarossa at Legnano in 1176, and the Peace of Constance of 1183 recognised the cities’ right to elect their own consuls.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Legnano, between Milan and Como, the forces of the <b>Lombard League</b> defeated the army of the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-29">29 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
